WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.650
How about figuring out how
to motivate the students?

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.480
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here today to
talk again about language learning and to

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:15.330
talk about how languages should be taught
in school with particular reference to an

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:20.130
article that appeared in The Guardian from
the UK, a leading newspaper in the UK.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.090
Remember if you enjoy these videos, please
subscribe, uh, click on the bell for

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:31.260
notifications and uh, if you're following
me on your podcast, please leave a review.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.450
So this, um, article, cause I
follow things in the news, uh,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.620
related to language learning.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.120
So here was this article in The
Guardian about the recommendation

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:48.420
from a body in the UK, that concerns
itself with education, that...

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.010
suggesting that a new approach to
language learning, one that would

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:59.070
sort of inject some more enthusiasm
into language learning would be to

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:06.570
have a list of 1700 basic words that
students would be required to learn.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.720
And this of course brought a tremendous
sort of wave of criticism from people who

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.300
are involved with language teaching saying
you can't just, uh, learn from lists.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:19.740
That's going to discourage people.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.350
That's not going to create a sort of a
sense of love of learning a language.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.910
So I, I sort of thought about this and
I did a lot of reading on the subject.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.770
So first of all, there is a bit
of a crisis surrounding language

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:39.450
learning in the UK, even though they
are just across the Channel from

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:46.470
France, not far away from Spain, uh,
not to mention, uh, other countries.

00:01:47.625 --> 00:01:52.725
Uh, not to mention the fact that we can
easily connect with, with China, with,

00:01:52.785 --> 00:01:56.384
uh, with the Middle East and learn all
these different languages and yet interest

00:01:56.384 --> 00:01:58.335
in language learning is declining.

00:01:58.725 --> 00:02:04.755
And I did some research and, uh, I
think more than 50% of schools give

00:02:04.755 --> 00:02:10.574
45 minutes a week of instructional
time to language learning.

00:02:10.574 --> 00:02:13.875
So very little time is spent
with language learning, interest

00:02:13.875 --> 00:02:15.285
in languages is declining.

00:02:16.305 --> 00:02:20.685
Uh, one of the reasons given
is that students think that,

00:02:20.745 --> 00:02:22.965
um, languages are difficult.

00:02:23.325 --> 00:02:27.705
So it's a risky subject to take
up because you may not do well.

00:02:28.095 --> 00:02:30.885
And so that might affect
your chances of getting into

00:02:30.885 --> 00:02:32.565
university and so forth and so on.

00:02:33.435 --> 00:02:34.545
So I thought about this.

00:02:34.575 --> 00:02:40.335
So on the one hand, of course, it's
hard to learn from a list, but on the

00:02:40.335 --> 00:02:43.755
other hand, I thought, you know, if
you have a very concrete, specific

00:02:43.755 --> 00:02:50.610
goal, you have to learn 1700 words,
then it's very clear, what you have to

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:58.320
do in order to get grades on language,
whichever German, Spanish, French.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:04.290
Um, and I think there's something to
be said for that, because I think now

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.200
a lot of people don't know why they're
even bothering to learn the language.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.940
They don't know what
they're supposed to achieve.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.135
Uh, Unfortunately, this recommendation
also suggested that the students

00:03:15.555 --> 00:03:19.125
be required to use these words
and to use them correctly.

00:03:19.185 --> 00:03:20.925
That's much, much more difficult.

00:03:21.585 --> 00:03:29.715
Um, I think the objective of any language
program should be to make the students

00:03:29.925 --> 00:03:34.725
like learning the language, even if
they're not tremendously motivated

00:03:35.175 --> 00:03:36.765
to eventually use the language.

00:03:37.765 --> 00:03:43.410
If they like the process because it's
very clear cut "I have to learn 1700 words

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:44.550
and I'm going to go about learning them"

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:50.070
um, that I think it's possible
might induce more people to get

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.670
interested in, in language because
the task is kind of finite.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:54.690
Yeah, I can do that.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.230
If I go at it for five years,
I can learn 1700 words.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.519
Uh, so on that level, it's not a bad idea.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:09.330
It's not a good idea, in my opinion,
to demand grammatical accuracy from

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:14.400
language learners in school, because so
few of them end up speaking the language.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.630
What does it matter whether
they are able to nail niceties

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.480
of grammar at the age of 12?

00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:22.980
To me, it doesn't matter.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.960
Uh, if there's some way of making
them enjoy the process of language

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:33.465
learning so that they expose themselves
to the language, they develop some

00:04:33.465 --> 00:04:36.975
degree of comprehension, they develop
a certain level of vocabulary.

00:04:37.215 --> 00:04:38.445
That's good enough.

00:04:38.835 --> 00:04:42.195
And some of those people will
be motivated enough to take

00:04:42.195 --> 00:04:44.805
it to another level right now.

00:04:45.525 --> 00:04:52.635
Very few relatively few graduates
of schools in the UK end up speaking

00:04:52.785 --> 00:04:57.435
another language, the lowest level
of any country in the European Union.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:58.590
They're not...

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.410
no longer in the European
Union, say in Europe.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:05.790
And I don't think the situation is
much different in North America.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:11.760
So, but where I think they've gone,
they've got it wrong with this list

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:18.540
of 1700 keywords that you must learn
is does let them learn any 1700 words.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:21.060
Why should you determine which
words they have to learn?

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.400
And if you set it up so that they
learn these words through a process

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.275
of listening and reading, like LingQ
where you're looking at words and

00:05:31.275 --> 00:05:35.205
saving words, and we can keep track of
the words you've learned, and we can

00:05:35.205 --> 00:05:40.485
even test you on your comprehension
of these words in context, using close

00:05:40.485 --> 00:05:45.435
tests, using multiple choice and so
forth, flashcards, whatever it might be.

00:05:45.975 --> 00:05:49.875
Uh, but why say you have to learn
these words because one thing we know

00:05:49.935 --> 00:05:55.545
for sure is that the high frequency
words they're going to show up anyway.

00:05:55.545 --> 00:06:00.015
So the first even thousand words, if
you do a lot of reading and listening,

00:06:00.015 --> 00:06:03.105
you're going to get those words cause
you're going to meet them so often,

00:06:03.675 --> 00:06:08.715
apparently theoretically, you need to
meet a word eight times in order to

00:06:08.715 --> 00:06:11.475
have a 50% chance of remembering it.

00:06:11.775 --> 00:06:13.905
So that's going to happen
for the first thousand words.

00:06:14.325 --> 00:06:19.225
And then if you look at any analysis
of frequency, frequency drops off so

00:06:19.225 --> 00:06:25.245
fast that for the next thousand words,
so that might get us closer to where

00:06:25.605 --> 00:06:30.195
this, uh, British, uh, educational,
uh, you know, authority was proposing

00:06:30.195 --> 00:06:35.445
1700 words, you end up having to read
two or three times as much in order

00:06:35.445 --> 00:06:38.925
to have a chance of meeting these
words often enough to remember them.

00:06:39.825 --> 00:06:40.755
Besides which...

00:06:40.785 --> 00:06:43.305
pretty much it drops off so quickly
that it doesn't really matter

00:06:43.305 --> 00:06:47.235
whether you learn the next thousand
or the next, the, the thousand

00:06:47.235 --> 00:06:49.185
after that, they're all a lot less.

00:06:50.175 --> 00:06:56.085
And so what matters much more is
what subject matter are you covering?

00:06:56.325 --> 00:06:59.925
If you are interested in sports,
there are certain words that

00:06:59.925 --> 00:07:01.365
will appear more frequently.

00:07:01.575 --> 00:07:05.955
If you're interested in economics,
other words will appear more frequently

00:07:06.465 --> 00:07:10.935
and as long as you have 2000 words,
in other words, if you have exposed

00:07:10.935 --> 00:07:15.735
yourself to enough of the language,
listening and reading that you

00:07:15.795 --> 00:07:22.035
actually know 2000 or 1700 words,
whichever words they are good enough.

00:07:22.065 --> 00:07:26.685
Now I'm going to get into the difference
between word families and words.

00:07:26.715 --> 00:07:28.034
However you want to count them.

00:07:28.034 --> 00:07:30.974
If it's 1700 word families,
maybe it's 5,000 words.

00:07:31.215 --> 00:07:32.114
It doesn't matter.

00:07:32.205 --> 00:07:37.455
Some numbers, some arbitrary number is
what a student is responsible to learn.

00:07:37.875 --> 00:07:43.515
Not necessarily this specific list of
words, but a specific number of words.

00:07:43.875 --> 00:07:48.674
And you go on read whatever you want
to read and listen to in that language.

00:07:49.424 --> 00:07:51.195
And we will test you on it.

00:07:51.255 --> 00:07:54.930
We'll test you on comprehension
and if you do okay, then you're

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:56.010
considered to have passed.

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:59.400
In fact, I wouldn't even test them
on comprehension in terms of, you

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.580
know, answering questions about what
happened and something they read,

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.170
because that's a different skill.

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:07.080
Again, that's the skill of trying
to remember what you heard.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:12.090
Uh, if we just testing them on say
the objective is to get you to where

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:18.120
you recognize in context, 2000,
3000 words, we test you on that.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.140
You do that.

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:21.860
That's, what's going to give
you your mark in the language.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:26.370
That might give the learners our
greater sense of achievement.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.810
Those people who are inclined, those
learners, who are inclined to get

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:35.429
enthusiastic about the language
through their listening and reading

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:39.030
through that sense of achievement,
of, of understanding more and more

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:43.980
of a language of enjoying content
of interesting of interest to them.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.740
Those people will take it further.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.540
And so with them, the objective
of, of turning learners into

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.100
enthusiastic learners will be achieved.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:57.660
Remember, there are two keys I often talk
about three, but let's talk about two

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.310
fundamental keys of language learning.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:03.449
One is the attitude of the learner, the
enthusiasm, the interest, the motivation,

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:05.550
and the other is the time spent.

00:09:05.819 --> 00:09:11.100
So if teachers can motivate their
learners to spend the time for

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:15.420
whatever reason, because have a very
concrete task learn 2000 or 3000.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:21.570
Or because through learning these words
and phrases through a lot of listening

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:25.530
and reading, they have a sense of
achievement and they have this sense

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:27.300
of understanding and other language.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.200
And then they are interested in
learning more about the language.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:36.390
Then that again is a major, um,
you know, should be a major goal

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:37.980
of, of language instruction.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:42.600
I always have, whenever I talk about
how I learn languages, inevitably, the

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.250
language teachers who prefer to have...

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:49.470
and who always say, well, that's fine for
you because you're motivated, but I have

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.420
a classroom full of unmotivated learners.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.220
So therefore your system would never work.

00:09:53.579 --> 00:09:57.329
Therefore they do what they've always
done, which in fact, isn't very

00:09:57.329 --> 00:10:01.590
motivating, which is teaching grammar
and then testing them on grammar and

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:03.230
finding that they get six out of 10.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:04.489
And so therefore they're discouraged.

00:10:04.844 --> 00:10:10.214
So that's not motivated maybe rather than
saying, well, Steve you're motivated.

00:10:10.214 --> 00:10:13.814
So your system works for you,
but I have unmotivated students,

00:10:13.814 --> 00:10:14.694
therefore it won't work.

00:10:14.745 --> 00:10:19.305
How about figuring out how
to motivate the students?

00:10:19.964 --> 00:10:24.735
Maybe not all of them, but a larger
number than are motivated right now.

00:10:25.005 --> 00:10:30.375
And maybe with an approach like that,
we won't see this dropping off of

00:10:30.405 --> 00:10:36.390
interest in language learning, which
is a major issue in the UK, uh, and

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:41.910
probably in, in North America, despite
all of the enthusiastic teachers.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:43.140
I have met them.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:47.370
I've met them at the, uh, American
council of teachers of foreign languages.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:48.780
And there are lots of them.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.270
And yet the statistics are there.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:54.120
There's less and less
interest in foreign languages.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:55.890
So something is not working.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.510
One solution suggested here by
this, and I'm going to leave all the

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.840
relative links in the description.

00:11:03.719 --> 00:11:07.050
Is this idea of having a list
of words that you have to learn.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:12.959
It may or may not work, but obviously
what is, and this is despite

00:11:13.319 --> 00:11:16.050
years of research like studies.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.599
Whenever I go and search on
the internet, I find detailed

00:11:18.599 --> 00:11:21.749
studies about all different
aspects of, of language learning.

00:11:21.839 --> 00:11:25.439
Most of which refer to who what's
happened, what happens in a classroom as

00:11:25.439 --> 00:11:29.890
if that's the only place where you learn
languages, which of course it isn't.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:35.580
Uh, the classroom should be where you
turn people on to learning languages.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:39.300
They will learn them elsewhere on
their own if they're turned on.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:44.130
But despite 50 years of
research, all we see is declining

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:45.390
interest in language learning.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:50.190
We don't see increased success in
language instruction at school,

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:52.200
in the UK or North America.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:54.360
Maybe it's not so complicated.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:56.100
Maybe people shouldn't over-complicate it.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:12:01.140
Maybe they should just stick with what
Krashen says, and what I definitely have

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:02.610
experienced in my language learning.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:07.080
And that is if you can expose yourself
to enough of the language you're going to

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:12.240
learn, and some people will be motivated
enough to do that others won't be.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:17.370
And if they at least have a very concrete
task at school, you have to learn 2000

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:22.440
words and we're going to test you on it
in using cloze tests or  multiple choice.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:25.859
So then they have a clear idea
of what their expected to so.

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:28.275
Some people will only do that.

00:12:28.335 --> 00:12:34.125
And I think maybe as many people as now or
more will be motivated to take it further.

00:12:34.485 --> 00:12:37.995
And so with that, I'll leave you with,
I'd be interested in your comments on this

00:12:38.295 --> 00:12:42.885
and maybe a couple of videos where I've
talked a bit about motivation and input

00:12:42.885 --> 00:12:44.835
and how that helps us learn languages.

00:12:44.865 --> 00:12:45.745
Thank you for listening.

00:12:46.064 --> 00:12:46.564
Bye for now.

